The '''2022 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2021–22 UEFA Europa League]], the 51st season of Europe's secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 13th season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was played at [[Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán Stadium|Estadio Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán]] in [[Seville]], Spain, on 18 May 2022,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0264-10fe90612aa3-37b2bc77f89e-1000/ |title=UEFA Europa Conference League: Who enters and when are the games? |website=[[UEFA]]|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 December 2020 |access-date=3 December 2020}}</ref> between German club [[Eintracht Frankfurt]] and Scottish club [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]].

The final was originally scheduled to be played at the [[Puskás Aréna]] in [[Budapest]], Hungary. However, due to the postponement and relocation of the [[2020 UEFA Europa League Final|2020 final]], the final hosts were shifted back a year, with Budapest instead hosting the 2023 final.<ref name="ExCo 17 June">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |website=[[UEFA]]|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=17 June 2020}}</ref>

Eintracht Frankfurt won the match 5–4 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]], following a 1–1 draw after [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]], for their second UEFA Cup/Europa League title after [[1980 UEFA Cup Final|1980]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0275-152d4568b8fe-4c36a5db723e-1000/ |title=Frankfurt 1–1 Rangers (aet, Frankfurt win 5–4 on penalties): Trapp seals shoot-out success |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 May 2022 |access-date=25 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/may/18/eintracht-frankfurt-rangers-europa-league-final-match-report|title= Rangers suffer Europa League final shootout defeat to Eintracht Frankfurt|date=18 May 2022|work=The Guardian|accessdate=20 May 2022}}</ref> That 42 years of await broke the record of the longest gap between two UEFA Cup/Europa League titles, which was held by [[Feyenoord]] before, with 28 years ([[1974 UEFA Cup final|1974]]-[[2002 UEFA Cup final|2002]]). Frankfurt became the first German side since [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]] in [[1997 UEFA Cup Final|1997]] to win the competition. As winners, they earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2021–22 UEFA Champions League]], [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], in the [[2022 UEFA Super Cup]], and qualified for the [[2022–23 UEFA Champions League#Group stage|2022–23 UEFA Champions League group stage]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/61446268 |title=Eintracht Frankfurt 1–1 Rangers (AET, Frankfurt win 5–4 on pens) |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation|website=BBC Sport|date=18 May 2022|access-date=19 May 2022}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Stade Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán - Séville (ES61) - 2023-04-25 - 3.jpg|thumb|275x275px|The [[Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán Stadium|Estadio Ramón Sánchez Pizjuán]] in [[Seville]] hosted the final.]]
This was the first Europa League final to be held at the stadium. It previously hosted the [[1986 European Cup final]]. The city of Seville hosted the [[2003 UEFA Cup final]] at the [[Estadio de La Cartuja]]. Spain had hosted four other UEFA Cup finals (holding a leg in [[1977 UEFA Cup final|1977]], [[1985 UEFA Cup final|1985]], [[1986 UEFA Cup final|1986]], and [[1988 UEFA Cup final|1988]]).

===Host selection===
{{for|the details on the selection of Budapest as the original host for the final|2023 UEFA Europa League final#Host selection}}

An open bidding process was launched on 28 September 2018 by UEFA to select the venues of the finals of the UEFA Champions League, UEFA Europa League, and UEFA Women's Champions League in 2021. Associations had until 26 October 2018 to express interest, and bid dossiers must be submitted by 15 February 2019.

UEFA announced on 1 November 2018 that three associations had expressed interest in hosting the 2021 UEFA Europa League final,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0250-0c5119fbc7e9-b9de0907b594-1000--11-associations-interested-in-hosting-2021-club-finals/|title=11 associations interested in hosting 2021 club finals|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=1 November 2018}}</ref> and on 22 February 2019 that two associations submitted their dossiers by the deadline.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/024e-0f8e68aa43f5-6b3adef08395-1000--9-associations-bidding-to-host-2021-club-finals/|title=9 associations bidding to host 2021 club finals|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|date=22 February 2019}}</ref>

