On 17 June 2020, the UEFA Executive Committee announced that due to the postponement and relocation of the [[2020 UEFA Europa League Final|2020 final]], Seville would instead host the 2022 final.<ref name="ExCo 17 June"/>

==Background==
This was Eintracht Frankfurt's third final in a major UEFA competition, having lost the [[1960 European Cup final]] to [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] and won the [[1980 UEFA Cup final]]. Having become the first German team in a major European final since [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in the [[2020 UEFA Champions League final]] and the first UEFA Cup/Europa League finalist from Germany since [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] in [[2009 UEFA Cup final|2009]], they were seeking to become the first German side since [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]] in [[1997 UEFA Cup final|1997]] to win the competition. Their manager [[Oliver Glasner]] was seeking to become the first Austrian to win a European trophy since [[Ernst Happel]] in the [[1983 European Cup final]] and the first Austrian to win the UEFA Cup/Europa League.

This was Rangers' fifth final in a major UEFA competition, having won the [[1972 European Cup Winners' Cup final]] and lost both the [[1961 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1961]] and [[1967 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1967]] Cup Winners' Cup finals as well as the [[2008 UEFA Cup final]]. Having become the first Scottish club in any European final since themselves in 2008, they were seeking to become the first Scottish club since [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] in the [[1983 European Super Cup]] to win a European trophy and the first Scottish side to win the UEFA Cup/Europa League. Their manager [[Giovanni van Bronckhorst]] was seeking to become the first Dutch to win a European trophy since [[Dick Advocaat]] with [[FC Zenit Saint Petersburg|Zenit Saint Petersburg]] in the [[2008 UEFA Super Cup]]; they were qualified after defeating Rangers in the year's UEFA Cup final.<ref>{{cite web|title= 2022 UEFA Europa League Final |url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2022/e/e_2033450_pk.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220517115214/https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2022/e/e_2033450_pk.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=17 May 2022|date=18 May 2022|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=18 May 2022}}</ref>

The two sides previously met twice in European competitions, in the [[1959–60 European Cup]] [[1959–60 European Cup#Semi-finals|semi-finals]], with Eintracht Frankfurt winning both legs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0272-1459a9ceeb09-fa22bad3bf99-1000--uefa-europa-league-final-head-to-head-record/|title=UEFA Europa League final: head-to-head records|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=5 May 2022}}</ref>

